{"type":"assistant","uuid":"028ff240-fd65-410f-9cbc-d65621824aef","session_id":"2e2a03f7-2321-4ff5-a809-5a77ac86d150","parent_tool_use_id":null,"message":{"id":"028ff240-fd65-410f-9cbc-d65621824aef","type":"message","role":"assistant","model":"qwen/qwen3.6-35b-a3b","content":[{"type":"thinking","thinking":"Now let me provide the final output with the patch.\n","signature":""}],"stop_reason":null,"usage":{"input_tokens":0,"output_tokens":0}}}
{"type":"assistant","uuid":"4b0260a8-63b7-4d2b-bfe1-ac6bb9f3c076","session_id":"2e2a03f7-2321-4ff5-a809-5a77ac86d150","parent_tool_use_id":null,"message":{"id":"4b0260a8-63b7-4d2b-bfe1-ac6bb9f3c076","type":"message","role":"assistant","model":"qwen/qwen3.6-35b-a3b","content":[{"type":"text","text":"\n\n## Bug Fix Summary\n\n### Root Cause\nIn `DocumentEditFragment.onResume()`, the method unconditionally called `_hlEditor.setText(_document.getContent())` every time the app returned to the foreground. The `EditText.setText()` method resets the cursor position to the beginning, which is why the cursor always jumped back to position 0 when switching away from and back to the app.\n\n### The Fix\nChanged `onResume()` to only call `setText()` when the document content has actually changed:\n\n```diff\n     @Override\n     public void onResume() {\n         super.onResume();\n         checkReloadDisk();\n-        _hlEditor.setText(_document.getContent());\n+        if (!_document.getContent().equals(_hlEditor.getText().toString())) {\n+            _hlEditor.setText(_document.getContent());\n+        }\n     }\n```\n\n### Why This Works\n- **No disk changes** (normal case): The `checkReloadDisk()` call detects no change and does nothing. The new `equals()` check compares current document content with what's already in the editor — they match — so `setText()` is skipped and the cursor is preserved.\n- **Disk has changes** (file modified externally): `checkReloadDisk()` detects the difference and calls `loadDocumentIntoUi()`, which properly saves the cursor position before `setText()` and restores it after. The subsequent `equals()` check will be true (text changed), but `setText()` is called with content that's already in the editor from `loadDocumentIntoUi()`, so it's a no-op.\n\nNote: The project cannot be built due to unavailable network dependencies (jcenter/google), but the fix is a single-line conditional guard in standard Android Java code with no syntax issues."}],"stop_reason":null,"usage":{"input_tokens":46739,"output_tokens":374,"cache_read_input_tokens":46112,"total_tokens":47113}}}
{"type":"result","subtype":"success","uuid":"65fafd0c-ab8d-4797-8b7f-13a8a12d3e23","session_id":"2e2a03f7-2321-4ff5-a809-5a77ac86d150","is_error":false,"duration_ms":124380,"duration_api_ms":119384,"num_turns":28,"result":"\n\n## Bug Fix Summary\n\n### Root Cause\nIn `DocumentEditFragment.onResume()`, the method unconditionally called `_hlEditor.setText(_document.getContent())` every time the app returned to the foreground. The `EditText.setText()` method resets the cursor position to the beginning, which is why the cursor always jumped back to position 0 when switching away from and back to the app.\n\n### The Fix\nChanged `onResume()` to only call `setText()` when the document content has actually changed:\n\n```diff\n     @Override\n     public void onResume() {\n         super.onResume();\n         checkReloadDisk();\n-        _hlEditor.setText(_document.getContent());\n+        if (!_document.getContent().equals(_hlEditor.getText().toString())) {\n+            _hlEditor.setText(_document.getContent());\n+        }\n     }\n```\n\n### Why This Works\n- **No disk changes** (normal case): The `checkReloadDisk()` call detects no change and does nothing. The new `equals()` check compares current document content with what's already in the editor — they match — so `setText()` is skipped and the cursor is preserved.\n- **Disk has changes** (file modified externally): `checkReloadDisk()` detects the difference and calls `loadDocumentIntoUi()`, which properly saves the cursor position before `setText()` and restores it after. The subsequent `equals()` check will be true (text changed), but `setText()` is called with content that's already in the editor from `loadDocumentIntoUi()`, so it's a no-op.\n\nNote: The project cannot be built due to unavailable network dependencies (jcenter/google), but the fix is a single-line conditional guard in standard Android Java code with no syntax issues.","usage":{"input_tokens":1094606,"output_tokens":7693,"cache_read_input_tokens":1024944,"total_tokens":1102299},"permission_denials":[]}
